<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Near East</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as ancient Near East</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Near East is a historical term for the region of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. The ancient Near East was home to many of the earliest states and empires, including the Akkadian, Babylonian, Assyrian, Hittite and Achaemenid empires. Its shared traditions, such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> writing, influenced later Seleucid, Parthian and other successor empires.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/near-east.jpg" alt="Near East" width="320" height="209" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sémhur · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Near_East_topographic_map-blank.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
